faq:
  - q: "What are the strongest job markets in Texas in 2026?"
    a: "Texas has three distinct major job markets: (1) Houston — the energy capital of the world. Dominant sectors: oil and gas, LNG, petrochemicals, healthcare (Texas Medical Center is the world's largest medical complex), aerospace and defense, and engineering. Employers include ExxonMobil, Chevron, Shell, ConocoPhillips, Halliburton, Schlumberger, Memorial Hermann, Houston Methodist, and NASA JSC. (2) Dallas-Fort Worth — the corporate relocation capital of the last decade. DFW has attracted corporate headquarters from California and the Northeast: AT&T, American Airlines, Goldman Sachs Texas operations, Wells Fargo regional HQ, Toyota North America, Caterpillar, Charles Schwab. Finance, technology, telecom, and logistics are the dominant sectors. (3) Austin — Texas's technology hub. Dominant employers: Apple, Dell, Tesla, Oracle, Google, Meta, Amazon, and a dense startup and VC-backed company ecosystem. The tech market has corrected from 2021–22 highs but remains one of the strongest in the US."
  - q: "How does the Texas job market differ from coastal markets?"
    a: "Texas job markets have several structural differences from New York, San Francisco, and Los Angeles: (1) Cost of living advantage — Texas has no state income tax and significantly lower housing costs than coastal markets, which makes nominal salary comparisons misleading. A $150K role in Houston often provides materially higher purchasing power than a $190K role in San Francisco. (2) Industry concentration — Texas is heavy in energy, healthcare, logistics, and aerospace; lighter in venture capital, media, and fashion relative to coast markets. Candidates from coastal tech or media roles may need to adapt their positioning. (3) Relationship-driven hiring — Texas job markets are notoriously relationship-driven. Chamber of Commerce networks, alumni associations, industry conferences (OTC in Houston, South by Southwest in Austin), and personal referrals carry more weight in Texas than in coastal markets where LinkedIn applications are more common."
  - q: "What should Texas job seekers know about salary negotiation?"
    a: "Texas salary negotiation has two specific considerations: (1) No state income tax — when comparing a Texas offer to an offer in California, New York, or Washington, remember that Texas has no state income tax. A California $200K offer with 9.3% marginal state income tax is worth roughly $180K after state taxes; a Texas $180K offer with no state income tax is equivalent in take-home. Understand after-tax comparison before accepting a lower nominal number. (2) Relocation packages — Texas has seen large inbound migration from other states. Companies relocating operations (Oracle, Tesla, Apple) often negotiate relocation packages for out-of-state candidates that are not always proactively offered. Ask directly if you are relocating. For energy sector roles in Houston, cash compensation can be supplemented by annual performance bonuses that range from 10–40% of base, and these are negotiable as part of the offer package."
  - q: "What career coaching services are most valuable for Texas professionals?"
    a: "Texas professionals benefit most from coaching in three specific areas: (1) Energy sector career coaching — transitioning within or out of the oil and gas sector requires specialized knowledge of how energy company compensation, equity, and severance packages work, and how to position energy experience for roles in renewable energy, finance, or technology. (2) Corporate relocation preparation — candidates moving to DFW to join companies that have relocated from coast markets often need help navigating the different interview culture and local market dynamics. (3) Executive networking strategy — Texas hiring, particularly in Houston and Dallas, is driven by networks. Executive coaching that includes industry-specific networking strategy for Texas markets (OTC connections, local chamber networks, alumni associations) produces better results than generic job search coaching."
  - q: "How is Austin tech hiring different from San Francisco or Seattle?"
    a: "Austin's tech market has grown significantly but differs from SF Bay Area and Seattle in several ways: (1) Startup ecosystem is smaller and earlier-stage — Austin has a growing but smaller VC-backed company density than SF. The best startup opportunities are at Series A–C companies, not the dense pre-IPO ecosystem that SF offers. (2) Large tech company presence is real — Apple, Dell, Oracle, Tesla, Google, Meta, and Amazon all have large Austin offices. These are not satellite offices but significant engineering and operations hubs. (3) Compensation is lower than SF — Austin tech compensation runs roughly 15–25% below San Francisco equivalent roles, partially offset by no state income tax and significantly lower cost of living. (4) Remote-to-Austin moves are common — many Austin tech workers relocated from SF and Seattle and are still employed by Bay Area or Seattle companies remotely. This makes the local Austin labor market competitive at senior levels."
